course: dictionary
lesson: 645
title: Tiny living things: bacteria
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Bacteria are tiny living things. Each one is made of just one cell, and you need a microscope to see it. Even so, bacteria live almost everywhere: in water, soil, air and inside your body.
explain: Context helps you work out what bacteria are doing. «Rotten eggs smell because bacteria break down proteins with sulphur in them.» The clue is rotten: bacteria are at work when food goes bad, because they feed and grow on it.
explain: Bacteria are not all bad. Some are harmful germs that make you ill, but many are helpful. «Germs include bacteria, viruses and fungi; most bacteria are harmless or helpful.» When you meet the word bacteria, read the sentence to see whether it is talking about harmful or helpful ones.
fact: Bacteria are tiny living things, and each one is made of just one cell.
fact: You need a microscope to see bacteria, because they are much too small for your eyes.
fact: Some bacteria are harmful germs that can cause illness.
fact: Many bacteria are helpful, for example in your gut, in soil, and in making yoghurt and cheese.
fact: One bacterium is a single bacteria cell, and bacteria is the word for more than one.
exercise: «Rotten eggs smell because bacteria break down proteins with sulphur in them.» What are the bacteria doing here? | Glowing in the dark | Flying through the air | Turning into eggs | *Breaking things down
exercise: Which of these is a helpful use of bacteria? | Making yoghurt | *Making yoghurt and cheese | Painting pictures | Making ice cubes
exercise: Bacteria are too small to see with your eyes. What do you need to see them? | A telescope | *A microscope | A ruler
test: Antibiotics are medicines that kill bacteria. Which germs do they have no effect on? | Harmful germs in a cut | *Viruses | Bacteria
test: Which is true about bacteria? | They are not living things | They are bigger than an elephant | *Each one is made of just one cell | They can be seen without a microscope
exam: Sam says all bacteria make you ill. Is Sam right? | Yes, all bacteria are harmful germs | *No, many bacteria are helpful, for example in your gut | No, bacteria are not living things | Yes, but only in summer
